  <section id="abstract">
    <p>
      <strong>Aslanbek Sultanovich Mirzoev</strong>, Candidate of Historical
      Sciences, Senior Researcher, Sector of Medieval and Modern History,
      Institute for the Humanities Research – branch of the Federal State
      Budgetary Scientific Institution “Federal Scientific Center
      ‘Kabardino-Balkarian Scientific Center of the Russian Academy of
      Sciences’” (IHR KBSC RAS)
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Abstract.</strong> Enlightenment activism
      <sup><a href="#n1" id="r1">[†1]</a></sup> as a cultural phenomenon in the
      life of the Circassian diaspora abroad appeared almost simultaneously with
      the diaspora's formation in the second half of the 19th century. Several
      factors underlay this phenomenon: the desire to preserve national identity
      while living in a foreign-language cultural environment, the emergence of
      the diaspora's own intelligentsia, which had received a European or
      Islamic education, and the absence of any form of national statehood. To
      preserve the language, traditions and national culture, cultural and
      educational societies were created in the countries where the Circassian
      diaspora lived. The Circassian school in Quneitra
      <sup><a href="#n2" id="r2">[†2]</a></sup> was one of the lines of activity
      of the “Circassian Society for the Promotion of Education and Culture,”
      founded by a group of Circassian intellectuals in Syria headed by Samgug
      Amin.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Keywords:</strong> Circassian diaspora, Syria, Quneitra,
      Circassian school, cultural and educational activity.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="article">
    <p>
      The Russo-Caucasian War (1764–1864) led to the formation of a large
      Circassian diaspora outside the Caucasus. Expelled from the Caucasus, the
      Circassians who ended up in the Middle East found themselves in a
      different linguistic and cultural environment and faced the problem of
      preserving their ethnocultural distinctiveness. To preserve it and counter
      assimilation, Circassian communities began creating cultural and
      educational societies in the places where they lived. These societies
      studied national history and worked to preserve the Circassian language,
      traditions and culture.
    </p>
    <p>
      Even before the collapse of the Ottoman Empire and the formation of the
      Arab states, charitable, social, cultural and educational societies
      created by members of the Circassian community had begun their cultural
      and educational work. This was made possible by the political freedoms
      proclaimed after the victory of the Young Turk Revolution of 1908.
      National and religious minorities were guaranteed civil rights, and they
      were allowed to form societies and unions and publish newspapers. In 1908
      the “Circassian Society for Unity and Mutual Aid” (<em>Khase</em>)
      <sup><a href="#n3" id="r3">[†3]</a></sup> opened in Istanbul. From 1910 it
      published the Circassian newspaper “<em>Guaze</em>”
      <sup><a href="#n4" id="r4">[†4]</a></sup>. It also established contacts
      with the historical homeland and published books and primers written in
      the Kabardian dialect of the Circassian language using an Arabic-based
      script. The society's secretary and the newspaper's editor-in-chief was
      Nuri Tsagov [Khakuashe 1993]. In 1913 he returned to the historical
      homeland, to Kabarda, where he engaged in educational work. In 1917,
      together with the Dymov brothers in the village of Staraya Krepost (now
      the city of Baksan in the Kabardino-Balkarian Republic), he organized the
      publication of the newspaper “<em>Adyge Mak</em>” (“Voice of the Adyge”)
      in the Circassian language, in an Arabic-based script [Oshnokova 2003:
      13].
    </p>
    <p>
      Representatives of the Circassian diaspora in the Arab countries also
      carried out active cultural and educational work aimed at preserving
      Circassian culture, language, traditions and national self-awareness. In
      Syria this work was developed by a group of Circassian intellectuals
      headed by their leader Samgug Amin (1900–1953). He was born in Syria into
      an Abadzekh<sup><a href="#fn-abadzekh" id="r-abadzekh">*</a></sup> family.
      After finishing primary school in Quneitra and secondary school in
      Damascus, he qualified as an agronomist in Beirut. In the 1920s he then
      studied in the political economy department of the Sorbonne. During his
      years of study in France he collected scholarly material on the history of
      the Circassian people. He later used it as the basis for several works, of
      which two were published: “Introduction to the History of the Circassians”
      (Damascus, 1984) <sup><a href="#n5" id="r5">[†5]</a></sup> and “The
      Circassians in the Wars against the Tsars” (Amman, 1984). From 1928 to
      1936 he was chairman of the municipality of Quneitra, and in 1931 he was
      elected a deputy of the Syrian parliament
      <sup><a href="#n6" id="r6">[†6]</a></sup> [Adyge (Circassian)
      Encyclopedia 2006: 1036].
    </p>
    <p>
      In 1927 a group of like-minded people around Samgug Amin formed the
      “Circassian Society for the Promotion of Education and Culture.” The text
      of the society's charter, in the Ottoman language and published in
      Quneitra in 1928, was translated into Russian by B.N. Beresgov [Beresgov
      2017: 13–18]. The “Circassian Society for Upbringing and Unity” (a literal
      translation of the organization's name from Circassian – A.M.)
      <sup><a href="#n7" id="r7">[†7]</a></sup> set itself the task of carrying
      out comprehensive activity aimed at preserving the native language and
      customs. It also aimed to promote the development of national literature
      and to obtain from the local authorities and the representatives of the
      French colonial administration autonomy for the Circassian community in
      the Quneitra district. The society was run by a Central Committee of nine
      people, based in Quneitra. City and village branches of the society were
      created in the towns and villages where Circassians lived compactly. They
      were headed by elected leaders (<em>tkhamadas</em>)
      <sup><a href="#n8" id="r8">[†8]</a></sup> and subordinate to the Central
      Committee. The society's general assembly (congress) was held every year
      on 10 May in Quneitra. It approved the annual plan of activities and the
      organization's income and expenditure. 10 May was declared a national
      holiday [Beresgov 2017: 16].
    </p>
    <p>
      In Damascus in 1929 a Circassian primer, edited by Balag Naubatuk,
      appeared. It used a Latin-based alphabet [Circassian Public
      Associations…]. Brochures and school textbooks in the Circassian language
      began to be published. The new society also set up publication of the
      newspaper “<em>Mardzh</em>”
      <sup><a href="#n9" id="r9">[†9]</a></sup> (from 7 October 1927 to 1932),
      whose editor-in-chief was Khagur Tarik Mumtaz. “<em>Mardzh</em>” was
      published in four languages: Circassian, Turkish, Arabic and French. It
      therefore had four pages. The newspaper published material on the history,
      culture and various problems of the Circassian people in Syria and other
      countries. Issues were sent to the Circassian communities of Transjordan,
      Egypt and Turkey [Informant Bedanoko Nihat Hasan 2019]. On the basis of
      the Circassian Society for the Promotion of Education and Culture, a
      Circassian Literary Union was created, chaired by Salim Zakaria, along
      with various committees and a sports society. The society's activity
      continued until 1932, after which it was closed for political reasons
      <sup><a href="#n10" id="r10">[†10]</a></sup> [Kushkhabiev 1993: 119].
    </p>
    <p>
      In 1933, with the support of the French administration, with which Samgug
      Amin cooperated, a Circassian national school was opened in Quneitra. It
      was designed to teach children from the whole district [Kushkhabiev 1993:
      119]. The school was funded both from state funds and from private
      donations [Informant Zhyrchago Gisa Ayubovich 1995]. Samgug Amin and
      other patriots opened shops, whose income went to finance the school.
      Soldiers of the Circassian regiment of the French armed forces in Syria
      donated part of their pay to support the school and to publish the
      newspaper “<em>Mardzh</em>” [Informant Bedanoko Nihat Hasan 2019].
      Teachers were invited to teach at the school from various Circassian
      regions of Syria [Kushkhabiev 1993: 119]. They were Samgug Amin himself,
      Yahya Sherkas, Akif Khunagu, Fakhri Khazhu, Ismail Anzor, Rufat Gutoko,
      Sheikh-Tahir Dzhatao, Muhammad Thakakho, Hamad-Ali Iskhako, Yahya Khatko,
      Rajab and Harun Blanao, Abdul-Latif Udzhykhu, Adib Shaguzh, Ahmad Kat,
      Ibrahim Tsey and Sheikh-Said Shapsug
      <sup><a href="#n11" id="r11">[†11]</a></sup>. Among the teachers there
      were also women: Charlotte Gutuko and Sataney Libzu. Of these teachers,
      the poets were Samgukh Amin, Kat Ahmad, Tsey Ibrahim, Shapsug Sheikh-Said,
      Khazhu Fakhri and Harun Batoko (Blanao) [Informant Bedanoko Nihat Hasan
      2019; Hafitse 2000: 267–268]. The first director of the school was Rakhfat
      Gutoko, but he died in April 1933. Akif Khunagu later served as director
      [Informant Bedanoko Nihat Hasan 2019]. Besides general subjects, the
      school taught the Circassian language, folk customs, songs and dances.
      They tried to instill in the children a spirit of patriotism and love for
      the historical homeland [Kushkhabiev 1993: 119]. Every day classes at the
      school began with the pupils performing the Circassian anthem, whose words
      and music were composed by Samgug Amin [Informant Bedanoko Nihat Hasan
      2019; Hafitse 2000].
    </p>
    <p>
      Under the curriculum, instruction in grades 1 through 3 was conducted only
      in Circassian. In grades 4–5 Arabic was added. The Arabic teachers were
      Arabs, provided by the state; all the other teachers were Circassians.
      From grade 5 French was added. The French teacher was Charlotte Gutoko,
      who had been educated in France. Arabic and French were studied as foreign
      languages. All the pupils at the school were Circassians. The letters for
      the Circassian language were Latin. The type (font) for printing was made
      in the school itself, out of wood. Books were produced as
      <em>samizdat</em> <sup><a href="#n12" id="r12">[†12]</a></sup>. Everything
      was done by the teachers. Besides the school, there were plans to open
      vocational schools in three specializations: biological,
      commercial-economic and trades (industrial). Their graduates were to go on
      to continue their studies at universities in Syria and other countries.
    </p>
    <p>
      The founders saw the purpose of creating the school as raising Circassians
      in a national spirit and instilling in them knowledge of customs, folklore
      and history. In their conception the school's pupils were to become,
      figuratively speaking, “seeds for sowing.” They saw in them the future
      ideologues and practitioners of their people's return to the historical
      Homeland. For this, however, the pupils had to be ideologically prepared
      and well educated [Informant Bedanoko Nihat Hasan 2019].
    </p>
    <p>
      The pupils, boys and girls, wore national dress. A school orchestra was
      formed from the pupils. National dances were taught at the school. Every
      year cultural and sports festivals were held. At them pupils competed in
      reciting poetry in the Circassian language, knowledge of folklore, and
      performing national songs and dances. A school theater worked, staging
      short plays in the Circassian language. Much attention was paid to the
      pupils' physical training, and sports festivals with competitions in
      various sports were held. Two football teams of Circassians living in the
      Golan were created, “Yamlidzh” and “Yamyblyazh”
      <sup><a href="#n13" id="r13">[†13]</a></sup> [Informant Bedanoko Nihat
      Hasan 2019].
    </p>
    <p>
      Every year in the competition among primary schools, the Circassian school
      took first place. The teachers worked with great enthusiasm. The school
      operated until 1943. Several factors caused its closure. One of them was
      the start of the Second World War. Syria, declared a military zone, was
      placed under a state of emergency. The territory of the Middle East became
      a theater of military operations, and the French military units split into
      supporters of General de Gaulle, leader of the French resistance, and
      supporters of the French collaborationists, the “Vichyites,” who had taken
      the path of cooperation with Nazi Germany. The second factor was the
      strengthening of the pro-Arab faction among the Circassians, whose
      supporters grew in number in the early 1930s. It was headed by large
      landowners from the Circassian clans of Bag and Duguzh
      <sup><a href="#n14" id="r14">[†14]</a></sup>. “This faction,” as A.
      Kushkhabiev notes in his work, “maintained close ties with Arab nationalist
      parties and organizations… Emphasizing religious commonality with the Arabs
      and pan-Syrian unity, they opposed the creation of Circassian autonomy and
      called on their compatriots to support the Arab liberation movement,
      supported by Arab nationalists
      <sup><a href="#n15" id="r15">[†15]</a></sup>, who were fighting for the
      abolition of the French mandate and the creation of an independent Syrian
      Arab state” [Kushkhabiev 1993: 118].
    </p>
    <p>
      In September 1936 a treaty was signed between Syria and France recognizing
      the former's independence. The mandate was to be abolished within three
      years of the treaty's ratification. The strengthening of the Arab
      liberation movement also resulted in an offensive by the Syrian government
      against the rights of ethnic and religious minorities. On 9 September 1936
      an armed clash took place between two Circassian youth factions: supporters
      of Samgug's group and the pro-Arab organization “Iron Shirts.” There were
      casualties on both sides. The Syrian national government used this
      incident as a pretext for accusing the “Circassian Society for the
      Promotion of Education and Culture” of collaborating with the French. As a
      result, the society's further activity was banned [Kushkhabiev 1993: 120].
      Amin Samgug was removed from his post as chairman of the Quneitra
      municipal council.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 1937 Samgug Amin and his like-minded associates formed a new
      organization, “Jolan” <sup><a href="#n16" id="r16">[†16]</a></sup>, which
      aimed to promote the mass return of the Circassians to the Caucasus.
      Representatives of this organization appealed to the Soviet embassy in
      Paris with a request to grant Syrian Circassians the right to repatriation,
      but received a negative reply, after which the activity of “Jolan” was
      suspended. Amin Samgug withdrew from political struggle and continued only
      his scholarly work and teaching at the school until its closure in 1943.
    </p>
    <p id="fn-abadzekh">
      * The Abadzekhs were a Circassian sub-ethnic group who, before the
      deportation to Turkey, lived in the Northwest Caucasus in the basins of
      the Belaya, Kurdzhips, Psekups, Pshish and Pshekha rivers.
      <a href="#r-abadzekh">&uarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>
