  <section id="translation-notes">
    <h2>Translation notes</h2>

    <h3>Terminology and wording</h3>
    <ul>
      <li id="n1">
        <strong>†1 “Просветительство.”</strong> Can mean “enlightenment,”
        “educational activism,” or “the cultural-educational movement.” The
        author's own English abstract uses “Enlightenment,” which reads oddly in
        English. “Enlightenment activism” was chosen because the sense is the
        19th-century national awakening through education.
        <a href="#r1">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n3">
        <strong>†3 “Хасэ” (Khase).</strong> A Circassian word for a traditional
        council or assembly, kept as a transliteration.
        <a href="#r3">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n8">
        <strong>†8 “Тхамады.”</strong> In Caucasian usage this means an elder
        or leader, and also a feast toastmaster. “Elected leaders” fits the
        context, but the Circassian term is kept.
        <a href="#r8">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n12">
        <strong>†12 “Самиздат.”</strong> Likely “homemade, self-published,” with
        no implication of Soviet-style dissident publishing, which would be
        anachronistic here. <a href="#r12">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n15">
        <strong>†15 “поддерживаемых арабскими националистами.”</strong> The
        quotation from Kushkhabiev is grammatically broken in Russian. The
        participle is plural and does not agree with “movement,” so it is unclear
        whether the movement or the Circassians are “supported by Arab
        nationalists.” The translation is a best guess.
        <a href="#r15">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Names and transliteration</h3>
    <p>
      Everything below is transliterated from Russian, which in turn
      transliterates Circassian, Arabic and Turkish names. Standard English
      spellings may differ.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li id="n2">
        <strong>†2 Кунейтра</strong> is normally “Quneitra” or “Kuneitra” (the
        author's English abstract uses “Kuneitra”). <a href="#r2">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n4">
        <strong>†4 “Гъуазэ.”</strong> Rendered “Guaze”; may also appear as
        “Gwaze” (“guide/herald”). <a href="#r4">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n7">
        <strong>†7</strong> The author flags this name as a “literal translation
        from Circassian.” The society therefore has two names in the text,
        “Promotion of Education and Culture” and “Upbringing and Unity.” They
        are probably the same body, but the author never says so.
        <a href="#r7">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n9">
        <strong>†9 “Мардж.”</strong> Probably the Arabic <em>Marj</em>
        (“meadow”). Written “Mardzh” to stay faithful to the Russian.
        <a href="#r9">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n11">
        <strong>†11 Teachers' names.</strong> Spellings are inconsistent within
        the article itself: Samgug / Samgugh / Samgukh / Amin Samgug; Rufat /
        Rahfat Gutoko / Gutuko; Harun Blanao / Batoko. The teacher list gives
        “Sheikh-Tagir,” rendered here as “Tahir” on the assumption that it is
        Arabic <em>Tahir</em>, which Russian often renders as “Тагир.” Please
        verify. <a href="#r11">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n13">
        <strong>†13 “Ямлидж” and “Ямыбляж.”</strong> Football team names. The
        words could not be identified, so they are transliterated only.
        <a href="#r13">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n14">
        <strong>†14 “Баг” and “Дугуж.”</strong> Circassian clan names,
        transliterated only. <a href="#r14">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n16">
        <strong>†16 “Джолан.”</strong> Probably Arabic <em>Jawlan</em> (Golan).
        The text itself uses “Голаны” for the region.
        <a href="#r16">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Possible errors or inconsistencies in the source</h3>
    <p>These are not mistranslations.</p>
    <ul>
      <li id="n5">
        <strong>†5</strong> Two works are dated <strong>1984</strong>, though the
        author died in <strong>1953</strong>. They must be posthumous
        publications, but the text does not say so. <a href="#r5">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n6">
        <strong>†6</strong> The dates of his parliamentary term (1931) and
        municipal chairmanship (1928–36) are as given by the author and were not
        independently checked. <a href="#r6">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n10">
        <strong>†10</strong> The society is said to have been closed in 1932, yet
        it is later described as still active in 1936, when it was banned after
        the “Iron Shirts” clash. The chronology is therefore contradictory, or
        the 1932 closure was only partial. <a href="#r10">&uarr;</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        The school's first director is said to have died in April 1933, the same
        year the school opened.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Russo-Caucasian War is dated “1764–1864,” which is the author's
        chosen periodization. Other historians use different start dates.
      </li>
      <li>
        The 1936 Franco-Syrian treaty is described only briefly. France never
        ratified it, though the text speaks only of “ratification” in the
        abstract.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
